ng relative ones which  are sort of on a grand per gram basis so  you can compare foods that way or you  can compare on a typical serving size or  typical item size as well in addition in  addition to all of these nutritional  properties we have a lot of non  nutritional ones i like food types for  example which I'll cover in the next  slide and varieties and various things  with how these foods are all very  precisely organized and defined  and even we can make nice nutrition  labels out of them too so one of the  neat things we have is uh food types  which is which is a little fuzzier than  foods are which we need to have because  you know a hundred thousand foods are  really hard to navigate through so we  need about a much smaller fuzzy or sort  of thing to work with and these are food  types these do not have specific  nutritional information but they're  intended as sort of a navigation tool  and so they have a lot of non  nutritional properties as you can see  here so things like almonds bananas  apples oranges those sorts of things we  even have a lot of other things that you  might not even think or even related to  this stuff like even related Pokemon for  example so you can do lookups on these  and this is kind of how we sort of  navigate through the domains by using a  new energy value feature which was  introduced in Mathematica 10 called  implicit entities and so that's  basically instead of having a string to  represent a single entity in the second  argument we now allow a list of rules  there which allows us to sort of zoom in  on what we want so in this case we have  we want to find foods whose preparation  is refried and food type is beans that  will give us a refried beans implicit  entity and so basically this is one  entity that sort of aggregates over all  of them on you can always get them back  by using random entity or entities and  of course if you query for a single  value you get one value back that's the  nice thing about having implicit  entities and you can do a lot of other  cool things too like you can grab all of  the most calorie dense foods that we  have which are mostly fast foods  naturally and we also have a lot of neat  things you can do with image collage and  those sorts of things using this sort of  technology here another important tool  that we need to have is in the instance  which is how we represent quantities of  food so you can take any entity here and  then in the second argument you can  specify how much you have so this is  basically 200 grams of refried beans and  so the if we have properties that depend  on depend on the content on how much you  have that's what we use it for so here  we have absolute properties that sort of  you can feed it how much you had and  then that way it will scale  appropriately from there and you can  even make nutrition labels out of this  sort of stuff too and if we have the  ability to do this we  you to also be able to group things  together and the way we do that is by  using entity group I won't talk too much  about this but it's basically the same  thing it's a further layer of grouping  that way so you can still get back a  nutrition label that way so this sort of  pretty much wraps things up on what I  more or less showed last year I turned  out a lot of the examples and neat  things there because we have a lot of a  lot more new stuff to show off as well  but you can also see more if you have a  QR code reader feel free to take a  picture that'll take you straight to it  also you can download this notebook or  even the notebook that I used last year  just on passable so it should be right  on there as well one of the things I  point out in a blog post here is how to  compare apples and oranges with a nice  manipulate I I've left the code in here  if you want to see it but it's a it's  also on there to compare things and  there's a lot of other neat stuff like  comparing frozen pizzas and various  things like that but to let's move  forward onto the new packaged food type  stuff that we have so we've added a lot  more packaged foods and we're slowly  adding in more and more properties than  what we had before one of the first and  most important probably is marketing  descriptions so you can kind of see the  sort of trends that we have so one of  the first things you can do is uh here's  a couple examples of them and so I went  and grabbed one of the first things you  do when you do natural language  processing type stuff is to grab the in  grams and delete the stop words and  those sorts of things so that's what  these sort of are so these are sort of  the one grams here the single words and  these are the by grams repairs of words  and then work three words together or  the three grams is you might call them  and as you can see a lot of these are  nutrition based over here lots of  calories and an organic and those sorts  of things too and there's a lot of that  sort of stuff in these as well you see a  lot of Department in agriculture that's  because they were inspected for various  things from the US Department of  Agriculture and so the police stop words  gets rid of the of which is why that  shows up so since it is um pumpkin  season it's kind of nice to be able to  go through and find out what food types  are related to these and so by looking  up pumpkin you can find all the  different food types go with that and  make a nice word cloud out of it and you  can see the pub pumpkin frequently shows  up with baby food for example a lot of  other food types  well but uh also since its you know  pumpkin spice season as a lot of people  call it there's a lot of other foods  that are related to pumpkin spice here  as well that show up and of course you  can do this for other things like for  example organic and natural which am uh  organic mostly shows up for baby food  and a wide range of things and natural  shows up for even more things not just  one in particular and you may want to  look at other holidays too and it turns  out that they're not very exciting  Halloween pretty much there's only candy  and for Easter related things there's a  little more variation but you know it's  a little more variety there so moving  forward a little more we've added a lot  more international foods as well we've  added about 25,000 of them actually from  about 20 countries and so here's a nice  little jew region value plot that shows  off how many we have in each country in  Europe there's a lot of detailed  nutritional information for all these  things as you might expect I will make  this a little easier to see here so  there's just a couple here that we can  create a nice large Nutrition Facts  labels for and we also have translations  in multiple languages it's a little hard  to see here I can blow it up it doesn't  show up quite so well on here but we  have translations from the original  language that they were from as well as  those in English as well so we have all  of the English names up here since most  of our users generally speak English we  keep those here but we can also allow  access to the original names from what  from which they were translated with a  simple property qualifier which is shown  here so in order to do some of this  stuff when we deal with lots of foods we  need to have some sort of alignment with  these non nutritional properties that we  had before like food types and those  sorts of things it turns out a lot of  work like that for these international  foods has already been done for us which  is using a mod languid thesaurus which  has about 7,000 terms they're all very  detailed and translated in over seven  languages most of them  and they include cooking methods food  sources and those sorts of things and so  we're aligned to those almost we're in  the process of aligning everything we've  got into that but these foods at least  are tagged up with them and so you can  see here these are all individual tags  so they're a very precise and they all  have individual codes so that way  internationally you can just know what  code it is and then they're all  translated in that way some of the neat  things you can do with this is that you  can look up various foods much in the  same way that you can do with the  implicit entities otherwise in this case  here we've set one up where we can find  out which country in Europe has the most  cheese and it turns out that that's  finland on italy also has quite a few  more but there's some neat examples that  you can do here to just look do some  quick lookups that way you can also do  some nice nutrient comparisons like you  can look at the calorie density of foods  so you can kind of compare these and you  can kind of see there's there isn't  really any real trend but it is neat  that there are some on one end versus  the other and they sort of are vary from  one place to the next moving forward  from the international foods we also  have a lot of cooking equipment that  we've curated we've got about 2700  cooking equipment items and we were  slowly adding more and more physical  properties to all these sorts of things  so you can do some nice lookups for  example here we can grab the five  smallest knives that we have which are  Baker's knife primers on knife and those  sorts of things and you can also make  some nice visualizations and the clear  outlier here is the pizza knife which  naturally pizzas are pretty big so you  need to have a pretty big knife to be  able to cut them very easily so moving  forward on these we also have in  addition to that we have a lot of images  for all these things so you can make  some nice image collages as well as word  clouds and so the nice thing about this  is that you can make word clouds that  actually have tool tips of the images on  them so you can kind of see what they  look like so this is all part of our  sort of way to move forward to get into  recipes and those sorts of things this  is one of the pit stops 